  "textDifferences": [
    {
      "index": 22,
      "bound": {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s3-n17",
        "text": "A circuit can also have **forced electrical oscillations**.\nHere, an outside source supplies an electromotive force that changes periodically, repeatedly driving the charges.\nElectromotive force, or EMF, means the energy a source provides per unit charge; it is not a force.\n\nFor example, a rotating frame with a coil of several turns produces an alternating EMF in a uniform magnetic field (Figure 26).\nA turn is one loop of wire.\nIn a uniform magnetic field, the field has the same strength and direction everywhere.\n\nAs the coil frame turns, the magnetic flux through it changes periodically.\nMagnetic flux describes how the magnetic field passes through the area enclosed by the coil.\nAccording to the law of electromagnetic induction, the induced EMF also changes periodically.\n\nHere, the electrons move along with the coil frame, and the magnetic field exerts a force on these moving electrons.\nThis force makes the electrons move along the wire.\nThe rotating coil frame can therefore supply an alternating EMF to the circuit.\n\nWhen the circuit is closed, an alternating current flows through the galvanometer.\nThis instrument uses a pointer to show changes in current.\nAs the current repeatedly reverses direction, the pointer swings back and forth around its equilibrium position."
      },
      "current": {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s3-n17",
        "text": "A circuit can also have **forced electrical oscillations**.\nHere, an outside source supplies an electromotive force that changes periodically, repeatedly driving the charges.\n\nElectromotive force, or EMF, means the energy a source provides per unit charge;\nit is not a force.\n\nFor example, a rotating frame with a coil of several turns produces an alternating EMF in a uniform magnetic field (Figure 26).\nA turn is one loop of wire.\nIn a uniform magnetic field, the field has the same strength and direction everywhere.\n\nAs the coil frame turns, the magnetic flux through it changes periodically.\nMagnetic flux describes how the magnetic field passes through the area enclosed by the coil.\nAccording to the law of electromagnetic induction, the induced EMF also changes periodically.\n\nHere, the electrons move along with the coil frame, and the magnetic field exerts a force on these moving electrons.\nThis force makes the electrons move along the wire.\nThe rotating coil frame can therefore supply an alternating EMF to the circuit.\n\nWhen the circuit is closed, an alternating current flows through the galvanometer.\nThis instrument uses a pointer to show changes in current.\nAs the current repeatedly reverses direction, the pointer swings back and forth around its equilibrium position."
      },
      "whitespaceOnly": true
    }
  ],
